\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Measurement error in causal inference.}
Foundations appear in \citet{carroll2006, gustafson2004, buonaccorsi2010, bound2001measurement}, with DAG-based formalization by \citet{hernan_cole2009} and \citet{kuroki_pearl2014}. For binary variables, non-differential misclassification biases toward the null~\cite{bross1954misclassification, brenner1993, ogburn_vanderweele2012}, but this fails for polytomous exposures~\cite{dosemeci1990, jurek2005, veirod_laake2001}. Extensions address differential measurement error~\cite{edwards_lessler_naimi2021, imai_yamamoto2010}, endogenous misreporting~\cite{nguimkeu_denteh_tchernis2019}, mismeasured covariates under IPW~\cite{shu_yi2019a, shu_yi2019b}, mediation with mismeasured mediators~\cite{valeri_vanderweele2013}, nonclassical error~\cite{schennach2020mismeasured}, and multiply robust estimation~\cite{wei2025multiply}. All analyze a single DAG topology; ours characterizes how the \emph{same} confusion matrix produces qualitatively different bias across seven topologies.

\paragraph{Text classifiers and LLM annotation.}
\citet{grimmer2022} and \citet{feder_etal2022} provide foundations for text-as-data and causal inference in NLP. \citet{wood_doughty2018} first applied DAG reasoning to classifier error; \citet{teblunthuis2024} gave the most complete binary treatment ($K{=}2$, four DAGs); \citet{lee_wood_doughty2024} extended to categorical LLM classifiers but covered only confounding. On annotation quality, \citet{gilardi2023chatgpt} and \citet{ziems2024can} document rapid LLM annotator adoption (surveyed by \citealt{tan2024llm}), \citet{baumann2025} found 31\% incorrect conclusions, and \citet{kim_garg2025} showed 60\% error correlation across models~\cite{pangakis_wolken_fasching2023, cheng_mayya_sedoc2024, variance_aware_llm2026, hidden_measurement_error2026}. \citet{audinet2025} benchmark debiasing; \citet{keith_jensen_oconnor2020} survey the landscape. These works either restrict to binary labels, cover a single topology, or diagnose quality without connecting it to causal validity.

\paragraph{Sensitivity analysis and correction methods.}
Our ASV framework draws on sensitivity analysis without assumptions~\cite{ding_vanderweele2016}, E-values~\cite{vanderweele_ding2017, mathur2018}, robustness values~\cite{cinelli2020making}, and mediation sensitivity~\cite{imai_keele_yamamoto2010, pryzant_etal2021}, but parametrizes annotation error severity rather than omitted variable strength. \citet{lash2009} and \citet{blackwell_honaker_king2017} provide complementary frameworks; \citet{mahajan2006} and \citet{hu2008} establish identification and estimation under misclassified regressors, including nonlinear models with instrumental variables. For correction, PPI++~\cite{angelopoulos2023} and DSL~\cite{egami_hinck_stewart_wei2023} are topology-agnostic, with extensions by \citet{zrnic_candes2024, demirel2024prediction, cadei2025, gligoric2025}. We show this agnosticism is costly: correction effectiveness is itself topology-dependent, with matrix-based methods provably ineffective under linear FWL decomposition for four topologies.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We validate through Monte Carlo simulation ($K{=}3$), cross-$K$ robustness checks ($K{=}5,7$), and empirical validation with real LLM confusion matrices from three NLP tasks ($K{=}2,3,10$).

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

For each topology, we sample 100{,}000 confusion matrices from a Dirichlet$(1,1,1)$ prior, compute analytical probability limits, and validate against Monte Carlo simulation ($N{=}10{,}000$). ASV experiments use $C(\delta) = (1{-}\delta)I + \delta C_0$.

\subsection{Bias Taxonomy}
\label{sec:taxonomy_results}

\paragraph{Theory-MC agreement.}
Analytical probability limits match Monte Carlo estimates with MAPE of 1.6\%, averaged across 161 scenarios (23 LLM configurations $\times$ 7 topologies; per-topology range: 0.3\%--3.7\%).

\paragraph{Exposure DAG: sign reversal under multi-class misclassification.}
Under realistic accuracy (diagonal $\geq 0.7$), the violation rate is 13.7\% with 2.1\% sign reversal; at diagonal $\geq 0.9$, violation persists at 11.9\% (Appendix~\ref{sec:iv_dirichlet_subspace}). Under the full Dirichlet prior, 85\% of matrices violate the binary ``toward-null'' guarantee and 83.4\% cause sign reversal; $\min_k C_{kk} < 0.55$ predicts sign reversal with 91\% accuracy (Appendix~\ref{sec:sign_determinants}). The $K{=}2$ control confirms classical attenuation~\cite{brenner1993}, establishing the multi-class failure as structurally distinct.

\paragraph{Structural guarantee for non-exposure topologies.}
Five topologies exhibit zero sign-flip rate (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}): confounding, mediation, and front-door show 100\% amplification~\cite{brenner1993, dosemeci1990} but never reverse the sign; collider is protective ($|B(C)|$ non-increasing; Conjecture~\ref{conj:monotone}); M-bias shows weakest sensitivity (53.2\% violation). All patterns persist at $K{=}5,7$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:cross_k_robustness}).

\paragraph{IV: Wald estimator instability.}
The IV topology is the sole non-exposure exception: 52.6\% of random matrices cause sign reversal, stable across $K{=}3,5,7$. Diagonal dominance ($C_{kk} > 0.5$) eliminates sign reversal but preserves amplification (Appendix~\ref{sec:iv_dirichlet_subspace}); however, all 23 empirical LLM configurations show zero IV sign reversal.

